---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {"title": "Best MagSafe Power Banks", "url": "/best-magsafe-power-bank/"},
  {"title": "Best Hand Crank Radios", "url": "/best-hand-crank-radio/"},
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Power Stations",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Solar Panels",
    "url": "/best-portable-solar-panel/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Laptop Power Banks",
    "url": "/best-laptop-power-bank/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Chargers for Power Outages",
    "url": "/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-03", modified: "2026-11-03" });
const title = "7 Best Solar Power Banks (2026): Folding Panels vs Built-In";
const description =
  "The BigBlue 28W folding panel leads 7 solar power bank picks; a built-in cell makes only a watt or two, so filling a 10,000mAh bank from the sun takes days.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "bigblue-28w-solar-charger",
    "name": "BigBlue 28W Solar Charger",
    "category": "28W folding USB panel, 3 USB ports, SunPower cells",
    "badge": "Best Folding Solar Charger",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Highest rated output of the phone-sized folding panels on this list",
      "Uses SunPower cells, from a well-regarded cell maker",
      "Three USB ports, so a bank and a phone can charge at the same time",
      "Folds to roughly book size, with loops for strapping it to a pack",
      "Sold for years and easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The classic version has USB-A outputs only, so there is no USB-C fast charging",
      "Bulkier and heavier than panels in the 21W class",
      "Delivers well under its 28W rating outdoors, as every panel does"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy this folding USB panel when you want the most headroom for clouds, short days and a power bank waiting to be filled.",
    "description": "For years BigBlue’s 28W panel has been a go-to solar powered cell phone charger, and the reason is plain: it has more panel area than the 21W models, so it keeps more in reserve when the sun is weak. BigBlue lists SunPower cells and three USB-A ports. No USB panel passes its full rating through a phone cable, though. The 28W figure describes the cells under ideal lab light; after heat, angle and the 5V regulator, real output commonly settles at about half to two-thirds of it. Even so, this is the strongest tool here for the job that counts, filling a 10,000-20,000mAh bank over a long sunny day. Face it at the sun, plug a bank into it instead of your phone, and top the phone up from the bank in the evening. If a laptop needs USB-C Power Delivery, this panel is the wrong tool; look at a power station instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-powerport-solar-21w",
    "name": "Anker PowerPort Solar 21W Solar Charger",
    "category": "21W folding USB panel, 2 USB-A ports",
    "badge": "Best Lightweight Folding Panel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Slim and light enough to ride on a daypack",
      "Two USB-A ports with Anker’s PowerIQ charging",
      "SunPower cells, according to Anker’s listing",
      "A simple, proven design from a mainstream maker"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "USB-A only, with no USB-C output",
      "Less margin than a 28W panel in haze or low winter sun",
      "Listed under several Anker model names, which muddles searches"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The lighter, simpler folding panel for hikers whose main goal is keeping one phone alive.",
    "description": "Anker’s 21W folder is the lighter option next to the BigBlue, and many backpackers will find it the easier panel to carry. Anker lists two USB-A ports with PowerIQ detection, SunPower cells and a fold-flat fabric body with grommets for hanging it from a pack or tent. What you give up is margin. At a 21W rating, figure on something in the high single digits to low teens of real watts in good sun: enough to charge a phone directly under a clear sky, with less slack when clouds drift through or the sun sits low. The remedy is the one that applies to every panel here: charge a power bank, not the phone. The bank irons out the dips that make a phone stop and restart charging, and it stores the midday harvest for the night. To fill a 20,000mAh bank every day, move up to the 28W class; for a single phone on weekend trips, this panel is plenty."
  },
  {
    "id": "nekteck-21w-solar-charger",
    "name": "Nekteck 21W Solar Charger",
    "category": "21W folding USB panel, 2 USB-A ports",
    "badge": "Best Budget Folding Panel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Typically priced near the bottom of the 21W class",
      "Two USB-A ports",
      "The same fold-out, hang-it-up format as costlier panels",
      "A smart spare for a glovebox or emergency kit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "A less-known brand with a thinner support network",
      "USB-A only",
      "Shares the real-world output limits of every 21W panel"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible, inexpensive panel for an emergency kit; pay more only if it will travel with you often.",
    "description": "Nekteck’s 21W panel offers the Anker’s basic formula for less money: a fold-out fabric panel, two USB-A ports and loops for hanging it in the sun. For an outage kit or a car emergency bag that might come out twice a year, that is the right budget, because the rating and the physics are the same across the class. The savings come out of brand depth and accessory polish, not watts. Set expectations the same way as for any 21W panel: in strong, direct sun it charges one phone, and in mixed weather it fills a bank slowly. Pack it with a charged 10,000mAh power bank and a short cable and you have a solar portable charger that can keep a phone going through a multi-day outage, provided you get a few hours of decent sun. If it will hang off a pack every weekend, the Anker or BigBlue is worth the extra money."
  },
  {
    "id": "goal-zero-nomad-20",
    "name": "Goal Zero Nomad 20 Solar Panel",
    "category": "20W folding panel, USB plus 8mm output, built-in kickstand",
    "badge": "Most Rugged Panel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Integrated kickstand makes pointing it at the sun simple",
      "Feels sturdier than fabric-only folders",
      "Also connects to Goal Zero power banks and small stations",
      "Can be chained with other Nomad panels for more watts"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Weighs more than fabric 21W panels",
      "Generally costs more than generic panels of similar wattage",
      "The ecosystem perks only help if you own Goal Zero gear"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth paying extra for if you own Goal Zero gear or want a panel you can stand up and aim properly.",
    "description": "Of all the panels here, the Nomad 20 is the one designed as part of a system. Goal Zero rates it at 20W and builds in a kickstand, which matters more than it seems: a panel tilted toward the sun produces noticeably more than one lying flat, and most fabric folders have to be hung or propped on whatever is at hand. Alongside the USB port for phones and banks, it carries an 8mm output that plugs into Goal Zero’s Sherpa power banks and small Yeti stations, and Nomads are designed to chain together when you need more input. That makes it the stepping stone if a small power station might follow your phone setup. The costs are weight and price; per watt it runs higher than a BigBlue or Anker. If a phone is all you will ever charge, the cheaper fabric panels do the same work. If you want a panel that stands up properly on a tailgate or a rock, choose this one."
  },
  {
    "id": "biolite-solarpanel-10-plus",
    "name": "BioLite SolarPanel 10+ Solar Charger",
    "category": "10W panel with built-in 3,200mAh battery and kickstand",
    "badge": "Best Panel With a Battery",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "The built-in battery rides out clouds and saves a little charge for night",
      "Integrated kickstand plus a sundial for aiming",
      "Slim, rigid single-panel build",
      "A sensible balance between panel area and battery size"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Just 10W of panel, so charging is slow outside strong sun",
      "The 3,200mAh battery holds well under one charge for a modern phone",
      "Costs more than a plain 21W folder plus an inexpensive bank"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one all-in-one solar charger here with the right panel-to-battery ratio, though it is a small system.",
    "description": "For the convenience of a solar power bank without the make-believe, the BioLite SolarPanel 10+ is the honest choice. It is not a large battery with a token cell; it is a genuine 10W panel backed by a small 3,200mAh battery. That battery earns its place twice: it keeps current flowing to the phone when a cloud passes, so charging does not cut out, and it stores part of the afternoon’s harvest for later. BioLite adds a kickstand and a little sundial so you can face the panel squarely at the sun, which is the easiest free gain in solar charging. Keep the scale in view, though. Ten watts of panel charges slowly, and 3,200mAh falls short of a full charge for most current phones. It suits a solo hiker on day trips or a short overnight; for longer outings, a 21W or 28W panel with a separate 10,000mAh bank gives you more for similar money."
  },
  {
    "id": "blavor-solar-power-bank-10000mah",
    "name": "BLAVOR Solar Power Bank 10000mAh",
    "category": "10,000mAh bank, Qi wireless, USB-C, small built-in solar cell",
    "badge": "Best Built-In Solar Power Bank",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Tough rubberized shell that fits a pocket or glovebox",
      "Qi wireless charging pad on top",
      "A USB-C port next to USB-A",
      "Built-in flashlight for outages"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The solar cell is tiny, so a full solar recharge takes days of direct sun",
      "Charging it in the sun also heats the battery",
      "Wireless charging loses more energy than a cable"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A solid rugged 10,000mAh bank; buy it for the battery and think of the solar cell as an emergency trickle.",
    "description": "BLAVOR is among the better-known integrated solar power bank brands, and this 10,000mAh model is a fair purchase if you buy it for the right reason. As a power bank it does well: a rugged shell, a wireless charging pad, USB-C plus USB-A, and a flashlight that earns its keep in a blackout. As a solar charger it is weak, and that comes down to physics rather than anything BLAVOR got wrong. A cell the size of a phone makes on the order of a watt or two in ideal sun, while a 10,000mAh bank holds about 37Wh. Even before losses, filling it takes dozens of hours of perfect sun, which in real life means several days on a dashboard or windowsill. Use the cell to win back a little charge when nothing else is available, charge the bank from the wall or a folding panel the rest of the time, and it will serve you well."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-335-power-bank-20000mah",
    "name": "Anker 335 Power Bank 20000mAh",
    "category": "20,000mAh USB-C power bank, no solar cell",
    "badge": "Best Bank to Pair With a Panel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Double the capacity of a typical solar power bank",
      "USB-C and USB-A outputs",
      "Takes a folding panel’s USB output for charging during the day",
      "From a brand with a long track record in power banks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No solar cell, so it relies on a panel or an outlet to refill",
      "Heavier than a 10,000mAh bank",
      "Not a laptop-class bank"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The battery half of the best solar setup: pair it with any 21W-28W folding panel.",
    "description": "This is the product that makes a folding panel worth owning. The Anker 335 is a plain 20,000mAh power bank with USB-C and USB-A and no solar cell whatsoever, which is exactly the point. Connected to a 21W or 28W folding panel, it absorbs the midday harvest while you hike or wait out an outage, then charges your phone at full speed over a cable at night, without the start-stop behavior of charging straight from a panel. At roughly 74Wh nominal, a full bank holds about three to four charges for most current phones once conversion losses are counted: a long weekend of normal use, or several days of careful use in an outage. Filling it from empty takes a full sunny day on a 28W panel, so in practice you add a third or half at a time. For laptop charging, step up to a USB-C PD bank instead."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Solar Power Bank",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-solar-power-bank/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do solar power banks actually work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "As batteries, yes; as solar chargers, only as a slow trickle. A cell built into the case makes roughly a watt or two in ideal sun, so refilling a 10,000mAh bank from solar alone takes days of direct light. For solar charging that keeps up, run a separate 21-28W folding panel into a plain power bank during the day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does it take to charge a solar power bank in the sun?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "From empty, a built-in cell needs several days of direct sun to fill a 10,000mAh bank, which is why manufacturers call solar a backup feature. Feed the same bank from a 21W folding panel and one clear summer day is usually enough. Clouds, low winter sun and careless aiming can stretch either figure a long way."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size solar panel do I need to charge a phone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Treat 21W as the practical floor for charging a phone in a sensible time; a folding panel that size usually refills a 10,000mAh bank on a good summer day. Move up to the 28W class for a 20,000mAh bank, two people or winter use. Panels under about 10W are slow and only suit light, occasional top-ups."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I charge my phone directly from a solar panel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, while the sun is steady and direct. Passing clouds are the catch: when panel voltage sags, many phones quit charging and do not start again by themselves. Putting a power bank between panel and phone avoids that, keeps the flow steady, and lets you charge the phone at full speed from the bank after dark."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to leave a solar power bank in a hot car?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is a bad idea. Heat speeds up lithium battery aging, and a dashboard in summer sun can run far hotter than battery makers advise. That is one of the best reasons to choose a folding panel instead: the panel sits in the sun while the battery stays shaded at the other end of a cable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many phone charges does a 20,000mAh power bank give?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 20,000mAh bank holds about 74Wh nominal. Conversion losses mean most deliver roughly 60-70% of that at the port, which works out to about three to four full charges for a typical current phone. Phones with larger batteries land toward the low end of that range, and older or smaller phones toward the high end."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are solar chargers worth it for power outages?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For short outages, a charged power bank usually covers you and solar adds little. After major storms, when outages run long, a folding panel with a 20,000mAh bank can keep phones and a radio running indefinitely on a few hours of sun a day. Bigger loads, such as a fridge or CPAP, call for a power station."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "best solar power bank",
      "solar portable charger",
      "solar power bank charger",
      "solar powered cell phone charger",
      "solar panel battery bank",
      "folding usb solar charger"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          Best Solar Power Bank
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          For most people the best solar power bank is really two products: the BigBlue 28W folding panel feeding a plain 20,000mAh bank, because a cell built into a bank makes only a watt or two in ideal sun. Your hours of sun, and whether a laptop or CPAP is on the list, change the answer. Below: seven picks, sizing math, field tips and mistakes to avoid.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          Based on manufacturer panel wattage and battery capacity ratings, compared against typical real-world solar output. No paid placements. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">12 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>The Best Solar Power Banks</span></a>
              <a href="#how-it-works" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>How Solar Charging Works</span></a>
              <a href="#built-in-vs-folding" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Built-In Cell or Folding Panel?</span></a>
              <a href="#clear-day" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Charges per Sunny Day</span></a>
              <a href="#sizing" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Sizing Panel and Battery</span></a>
              <a href="#field-output" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Getting More Output</span></a>
              <a href="#outage-vs-trail" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Outages, Hiking and Camping</span></a>
              <a href="#step-up" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>When to Buy a Station</span></a>
              <a href="#which-to-buy" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Which One to Buy</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The little solar cell on the lid of a power bank is too small to matter: in real sun it adds a few percent a day. To keep a phone running on sunlight, pair a folding USB panel with a plain battery bank, and count any built-in cell as a bonus.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Best Solar Power Bank: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy the BigBlue 28W Solar Charger and the Anker 335 Power Bank 20000mAh as a pair: the panel collects, the bank stores, and the phone charges from the bank at night. Backpackers should swap in the lighter Anker 21W, emergency kits can use the cheaper Nekteck 21W, and the BioLite SolarPanel 10+ is the one all-in-one with a sensible ratio. Treat a built-in cell, like the BLAVOR’s, as an emergency trickle. Ratings below are the makers’ figures.
          </p>

