        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Does Solar Phone Charging Actually Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A solar charger turns sunlight into a few watts of electricity and pushes them through a USB regulator at 5 volts, whether it is a folding panel or a solar power bank charger with cells on the lid. The number printed on the box is the panel’s rating under standard lab light, which is bright, cool and perfectly aimed. Outdoors you seldom get all three together, so real output always lands below the label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much energy does a phone need?</h3>

          <p>
            Most current phones carry somewhere around 12-20 watt-hours of battery. Power banks are rated in milliamp-hours at a cell voltage of about 3.7V, which puts a 10,000mAh bank at roughly 37Wh and a 20,000mAh bank at roughly 74Wh. Once conversion losses are counted, a 10,000mAh bank usually delivers one and a half to two full phone charges, and a 20,000mAh bank three to four. Those are the figures sunlight has to replace.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much energy does the sun deliver in a day?</h3>

          <p>
            The handiest planning unit is the peak sun hour: one hour of light at the intensity panels are rated against. A clear summer day across much of the U.S. supplies four to six peak sun hours, while winter and cloudy days may give only one or two. Multiply the panel’s watts by the peak sun hours, take off about a third for heat, angle and USB conversion, and what is left is a realistic daily harvest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is a folding panel about ten times better?</h3>

          <p>
            Apply that to a 21W folding panel on a decent summer day: 21W times five sun hours is 105Wh on paper, or about 50-70Wh at the USB port. That is enough to fill a 10,000mAh bank and still charge a phone. Apply it to a solar power bank whose built-in cell produces one to two watts and the same day yields perhaps 5-10Wh, before the heat of sitting in direct sun starts working against the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap, roughly ten to one, explains the whole category. It is why the picks lean toward folding panels and batteries, with only one integrated solar power bank on the list. For the broader question of keeping devices alive when the grid fails, our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers batteries without the solar angle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="built-in-vs-folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is a Built-In Solar Panel Better Than a Folding Panel?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. A folding panel paired with a separate bank beats a built-in cell on output, battery life and flexibility. When people search for a solar portable charger, the integrated solar power bank is usually what they picture, and it is the version most likely to let them down. The appeal is easy to see: one object, no cables, leave it on the dashboard and forget it. The trouble is surface area. A cell the size of a phone screen cannot gather much light, and no cell technology closes that gap. Manufacturers generally admit as much in the fine print, where solar input is described as an emergency or backup feature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does heat matter so much?</h3>

          <p>
            Heat is the quieter problem. Lithium cells dislike high temperatures, yet the place a solar power bank charges best is hot, direct sun, often a car dashboard that climbs far past what battery makers recommend. You end up trading battery lifespan for a trickle of charge. A folding panel sidesteps that, because the battery waits in the shade at the end of a cable while the panel takes the heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a panel-and-bank pair fix?</h3>

          <p>
            Splitting the job solves both problems at once. The panel can be ten to twenty times the size of a built-in cell and still fold to roughly the size of a hardcover book, while the bank can be whatever capacity suits you. Each half can also be replaced on its own: when the battery wears out after a few hundred cycles, the panel keeps working, and if you later buy a small power station, a good USB panel still has a job to do.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Let the panel catch the sun and the bank carry the night.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Count a built-in cell as a bonus, never as the charging plan. If solar is the reason you are shopping, budget for a 21-28W folding panel and a 10,000-20,000mAh bank, and charge the phone from the bank.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do the two hybrids fit?</h3>

          <p>
            The two hybrids on this list sit at opposite ends. The BioLite pairs a real panel with a small battery, so it is a genuine solar system, just a modest one. The BLAVOR pairs a real battery with a token panel, so it is a power bank with a solar emergency mode. Both are sound products that answer different questions, and the BLAVOR is the one shoppers most often buy while expecting it to behave like the BioLite.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make integrated solar banks useless. As rugged, pocketable batteries with a flashlight and a last-resort trickle, they do the job, and the BLAVOR among the picks is a good one. Just buy them for the battery. If your idea of solar charging includes laptops, CPAP machines or a small fridge, jump ahead: that is power station territory, covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clear-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Phone Charges Can a Solar Charger Deliver in a Day?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a clear day with the panel aimed reasonably well, a 21W panel puts back roughly one bank or one or two phones, a 28W panel fills a 10,000mAh bank with room to spare, and a built-in cell adds only a small fraction of a charge. Here is how that harvest math plays out across the seven picks. These are estimates from the arithmetic in section 3, not measured results.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>BigBlue 28W:</strong> fills a 10,000mAh bank with room to spare.</li>
            <li><strong>Anker 21W:</strong> roughly one bank, or one or two phones.</li>
            <li><strong>Nekteck 21W:</strong> the same as the other 21W panels.</li>
            <li><strong>Goal Zero Nomad 20:</strong> similar to a 21W panel, and easier to aim.</li>
            <li><strong>BioLite SolarPanel 10+:</strong> about one phone charge.</li>
            <li><strong>BLAVOR 10000mAh:</strong> a small fraction of a charge.</li>
            <li><strong>Anker 335:</strong> no solar of its own, but it stores a full day’s harvest from a panel.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long does a full refill take?</h3>

          <p>
            It depends on which half of the system you are filling and from what. A 21W panel comfortably refills a 10,000mAh bank on a strong summer day. Filling the 20,000mAh Anker 335 from empty takes a full sunny day on a 28W panel, so most owners top it up a third or half at a time rather than running it flat. A built-in cell like the BLAVOR’s needs several days of direct sun to fill its 10,000mAh battery, which is why that kind of bank should be charged from the wall or a folding panel whenever you have the choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What cuts into the daily total?</h3>

          <p>
            Every estimate above assumes clear sky and decent aim. Clouds, a low winter sun, heat building up behind the panel and a panel left lying flat all pull the number down, sometimes sharply. Winter alone can cut the daily harvest to a third of summer’s. The next two sections cover how to size around those losses and how to claw some of them back in the field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Size a Solar Panel and Power Bank Together?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size the battery to cover the phone charges you need between sunny days, then choose a panel big enough to refill that battery in one decent day. Getting the order right matters, because a big panel feeding a tiny bank wastes sun, and a big bank behind a small panel never fills.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How big a battery do you need?</h3>

          <p>
            For one phone over a weekend, 10,000mAh is plenty. For two people, a week on the trail or a multi-day outage, 20,000mAh is the better starting point. Larger banks exist, but beyond about 26,800mAh you run into airline carry-on limits, and the extra weight starts to make the case for a small power station instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How big a panel do you need?</h3>

          <p>
            Work from the rough rule that a panel delivers half to two-thirds of its rating at the USB port. On that basis a 21W panel puts back around 50-70Wh on a strong summer day, and a 28W panel proportionally more. So a 21W panel comfortably refills a 10,000mAh bank, while a 20,000mAh bank is a better match for the 28W class, especially in spring and fall when the sun sits lower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What changes in winter?</h3>

          <p>
            More than most people expect. Shorter days and a lower sun angle can cut the daily harvest to a third of summer’s, and a panel lying flat on snow collects far less than one tilted toward the southern sky. If you need dependable solar charging from November to February, buy the bigger panel and spend time aiming it; the BigBlue 28W, or a Nomad 20 propped on its kickstand, is the realistic minimum.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do cables and extra ports matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. USB-A panels push 5V, and at that low voltage a long, thin cable loses a surprising share of the power. Run a short, decent cable from panel to bank. And if a panel has two or three ports, do not assume they add up to more total power; most share the same panel output and simply split it between them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Energy Math</p>
